---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import InstallCommand from "@/components/InstallCommand.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import pageMeta from "@/data/page-meta.json";

const meta = pageMeta["/docs/installation/astro"];
---

<Layout title={meta.title} description={meta.description}>
  <div class="docs-content">
    <p class="docs-eyebrow">Getting started</p>
    <h1>Astro</h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">
      Install loseta in an Astro project, load the generated CSS from a shared layout and import
      optional behavior through a processed client script.
    </p>

    <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
    <p>
      Current Astro releases require Node 22.23.4+ and do not support odd-numbered Node releases.
      The loseta CLI itself requires Node 22+. These examples use pnpm; the loseta command blocks
      also provide npm variants.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scaffold commands use <code>@latest</code>, so generated files can change between Astro
      releases. See the <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/install-and-setup/">official Astro
      setup guide</a> for current prerequisites.
    </p>

    <h2>Create an Astro project</h2>
    <p>Create the minimal template and install its dependencies:</p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code={`pnpm create astro@latest my-astro-project --template minimal
cd my-astro-project
pnpm install`}
    />
    <p>
      Accept the scaffolder's dependency and Git prompts as needed. If it installs dependencies for
      you, the explicit <code>pnpm install</code> is harmless.
    </p>

    <h2>Create a global stylesheet</h2>
    <p>
      The minimal template does not create a CSS entrypoint. Create
      <code>src/styles/global.css</code>; loseta will add its generated import to this file.
    </p>
    <p>Create a shared layout at <code>src/layouts/Layout.astro</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="html"
      label="src/layouts/Layout.astro"
      code={`---
import "../styles/global.css";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>My Astro site</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <slot />
  </body>
</html>`}
    />
    <p>Then use the layout from <code>src/pages/index.astro</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="html"
      label="src/pages/index.astro"
      code={`---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <h1>My Astro site</h1>
</Layout>`}
    />

    <h2>Initialize loseta</h2>
    <p>
      Point <code>init</code> at the stylesheet you just created. <code>--yes</code> skips prompts
      and <code>--import</code> inserts the generated stylesheet import:
    </p>
    <InstallCommand command="loseta init --yes --style nova --entrypoint src/styles/global.css --import" />
    <p>
      This creates <code>src/styles/loseta/index.css</code> and adds
      <code>@import "./loseta/index.css";</code> to <code>src/styles/global.css</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Add components</h2>
    <p>Add a CSS-only button and a behavior-bearing dialog:</p>
    <InstallCommand command="loseta add button dialog" />
    <p>
      The CLI installs the dialog's <code>dialog-shell</code> primitive and its behavior modules
      automatically. Pass <code>--no-behavior</code> if you only need the CSS.
    </p>

    <h2>Use the components</h2>
    <p>Add the markup to the layout's slot in <code>src/pages/index.astro</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="html"
      code={`<button type="button" class="btn" data-open-dialog="welcome-dialog">Open dialog</button>

<dialog
  class="dialog"
  id="welcome-dialog"
  aria-labelledby="welcome-title"
  aria-describedby="welcome-description"
>
  <div>
    <header>
      <h2 data-slot="title" id="welcome-title">Welcome</h2>
      <p data-slot="description" id="welcome-description">
        This dialog uses the CSS and behavior installed by loseta.
      </p>
    </header>
    <footer>
      <button type="button" class="btn" data-variant="outline" data-close-dialog>Cancel</button>
      <button type="button" class="btn" data-close-dialog>Continue</button>
    </footer>
    <form method="dialog">
      <button class="btn" data-variant="ghost" data-size="sm" data-close-dialog>Close</button>
    </form>
  </div>
</dialog>`}
    />

    <h2>Load behavior</h2>
    <p>
      By default, the CLI writes <code>behaviors/index.js</code> at the project root. Astro does not
      serve that directory at <code>/behaviors/index.js</code>, so import the generated index from a
      processed <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> in the shared layout:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="js"
      label="src/layouts/Layout.astro"
      code={`<script>
  import "../../behaviors/index.js";
</script>`}
    />
    <p>
      Use a processed client script, not component frontmatter. Astro bundles the import and sends
      it to the browser as an ES module.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-callout" role="note">
      <strong>Prefer a public script tag?</strong> Set <code>behaviorDir</code> to
      <code>public/behaviors</code> in <code>loseta.json</code> before adding behavior-bearing
      components, then load <code>/behaviors/index.js</code> from the layout. Changing the directory
      does not move files that were already installed.
    </div>

    <h2>ClientRouter and view transitions</h2>
    <p>
      Most loseta modules register delegated document listeners, so swapped-in markup works without
      re-importing the module. <code>message-scroller</code> and <code>resizable</code> also export
      <code>mount(root)</code> for late-mounted subtrees.
    </p>
    <p>
      With Astro's <code>&lt;ClientRouter /&gt;</code>, bundled module scripts execute only once.
      If you install either of those components, add these imports and the lifecycle listener to
      the same processed script. <code>astro:page-load</code> fires after the initial load and every
      client-side navigation:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="js"
      code={`import { mount as mountMessageScroller } from "../../behaviors/message-scroller.js";
import { mount as mountResizable } from "../../behaviors/resizable.js";

document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
  mountMessageScroller(document);
  mountResizable(document);
});`}
    />
    <p>Only import modules that your project has installed.</p>

    <h2>Run the project</h2>
    <p>Start Astro and open the local URL printed in your terminal:</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`pnpm run dev`} />

    <h2>Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/styles">Choose or switch visual styles</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/behavior">Read the behavior and lifecycle contract</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/components">Browse component markup and install commands</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/installation">Use the CLI reference for registry and cache options</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
